  "notes": "TRANSITIVITY: {他動詞|たどうし} (transitive). Pattern: Xを{撫|な}でる (to stroke X).\n\nASPECT: ている indicates ongoing action. {撫|な}でている = 'is stroking.'\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- {頭|あたま}を{撫|な}でる (to pat on the head)\n- {背中|せなか}を{撫|な}でる (to stroke one's back)\n- {髪|かみ}を{撫|な}でる (to stroke hair)\n- {撫|な}で{下|お}ろす (to stroke downward - also: to feel relieved)\n\nIDIOM: {胸|むね}を{撫|な}で{下|お}ろす - to feel relieved (lit. 'stroke down one's chest').\n\nCULTURAL NOTE: Stroking a child's head is a common sign of affection in Japan. The expression {頭|あたま}を{撫|な}でる often appears in positive contexts.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {触|さわ}る - to touch (neutral)\n- {叩|たた}く - to tap, pat (with more force)",
